{"summary":"Jason Ku introduces an algorithms course that will also be taught by Eric Domain and Justin Solomon. The course focuses on solving computational problems, proving solutions correct, arguing that they are efficient, and communicating those ideas clearly—often through writing. He defines a problem as a relation between possible inputs and correct outputs, commonly specified by a predicate that checks whether an output is valid. The course will consider general problems with arbitrarily large inputs and seek fixed-size procedures that produce correct outputs.","chapters":[{"title":"Course goals and expectations","start":"00:12"},{"title":"What counts as a computational problem?","start":"02:59"},{"title":"Specifying valid outputs","start":"04:48"},{"title":"General problems and scalable inputs","start":"06:07"},{"title":"What is an algorithm?","start":"08:56"}],"keyPoints":["The course emphasizes solving problems, proving correctness, arguing efficiency, and communicating solutions.","Students should expect substantial writing, not just coding.","A computational problem relates possible inputs to one or more correct outputs.","A predicate can specify a problem by checking whether a given input-output pair is valid.","Algorithms should address general problems and inputs of arbitrarily large size.","An algorithm is a fixed-size machine or procedure that takes an input and produces a correct output."]}